import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 599 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 600 | 2026-10-21 | **The Scale finder is reborn on the circle of fifths.** Her call (2026-10-21): a lookup and review tool for students who already learned, any order, built from the same parts as lesson 9, with the clock-tab feel. New `ScaleFinderDial` (the Toolbox card `scale-finder` now opens it): twelve major keys on the outer ring and their relative minors on the inner ring, a dial that turns (0.7 s, shortest way round) so the chosen key stands in a window at the top; labels stay upright; black keys show both names (sharp above, flat below); centre shows the name and the key signature; \"← ♭ / ♯ →\" steps, Major/Minor chips, tap either ring, or type a key (Bb, f#m). Under the dial: the keyboard with note names, 1 or 2 octaves of both hands' fingering (`ScPair`, now also able to show a minor key's fingering), the key signature line, the staff behind a button (key-signature notes green, signs coral; harmonic or melodic minors that need a double sharp leave the staff out with a note), twin spellings only where the old finder already had them (C♯/D♭ major, F♯/G♭ major, A♯/B♭ minor, D♯/E♭ minor), minor forms natural, harmonic, melodic (no finger numbers where the harmonic fingering would put a thumb on a black key, as before), \"Hear it up and down\" and a relative major or minor button. It still opens from the Home Scales clock (`PENDING_SCALE`) and from `?key=&mode=`. Registry blurb, Korean string, Home tool line and badge art updated. The old three-step finder code stays in the file for the legacy chapter page. Built by `patch609.py` (`t613`). Checked all 24 keys, all three minor forms, English and Korean, the URL entry and Home: no console errors. Local commit only, not pushed. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
